Development Server Speed Up (npm run dev)Enable Turbopack: Add the --turbo flag to your dev script in package.json (next dev --turbo).Clear the Cache: Delete the local .next folder and restart your terminal.Exclude Antivirus Scanning: Add your project folder to your OS antivirus exclusion list (e.g., Windows Defender).Exit Docker Container: Run npm run dev directly on your local host machine instead of a container to avoid filesystem lag.🌐 Production Server Speed Up (Reduce TTFB / Server Latency)Use Promise.all: Run independent data fetches concurrently rather than using sequential await statements.Implement Streaming / Suspense: Wrap slow data-fetching components in React <Suspense> boundaries to stream the page UI instantly.Use Static Generation (SSG/ISR): Pre-render pages to static HTML during build time instead of running Server-Side Rendering (SSR) on every click.Deploy on Vercel or Edge CDNs: Host your app on an infrastructure optimized for Next.js features to eliminate cold starts and server latency.Check Database Proximity: Ensure your database and your Next.js deployment region are physically located in the same data centre.📱 Client-Side Speed Up (Reduce Bundle Size & Page Weight)Optimize Images: Replace standard <img> tags with <Image /> from next/image to force auto-compression and lazy loading.Set Hero Image Priority: Add the priority attribute to images above the fold to boost your Largest Contentful Paint (LCP) score.Use Dynamic Imports: Wrap heavy libraries (like charts or text editors) in next/dynamic to load them only when required.Limit "use client": Keep parent layout architectures as Server Components; move the client directive deep down to leaf components (like interactive buttons).Audit Bundle Size: Run @next/bundle-analyzer to visually locate and remove oversized third-party packages.